<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Sendai Domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1600 CE – 1871 CE · capital Sendai</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The great northern domain founded by Date Masamune, third largest in Tokugawa Japan and leader of the northern alliance against the Meiji government.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/date-masamune">Date Masamune</a>, who had sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> in 1600, began building <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sendai-castle">Aoba castle</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sendai">Sendai</a> in 1601 and laid out the city around it. The domain was assessed at 620,000 koku, though its actual yield was much higher.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/date-masamune">Masamune</a> developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> land on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sendai">Sendai</a> plain and shipped surplus rice to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-tokyo">Edo</a>, and in 1613 he sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasekura-tsunenaga">Hasekura Tsunenaga</a> on an embassy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. A long succession crisis, the Date disturbance of the 1660s, ended in a famous hearing before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> in 1671. The domain had a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> class and maintained the Date family's cultural patronage.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1868 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sendai">Sendai</a> led the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ouetsu-reppan-domei">Ouetsu Reppan Domei</a> in defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aizu">Aizu</a> but surrendered that autumn, and its lands were reduced. After abolition in 1871 it became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miyagi-prefecture">Miyagi Prefecture</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sendai-domain.jpg" alt="Sendai Domain" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Kinori · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SendaiJoOtemonSumiYagura2003-11.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1600 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sendai</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Date Masamune, Date Tsunamune, Date Yoshimura</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Japan</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sendai han</dd></dl></aside></div>
